  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nEarly this morning GOES water vapor imagery and 500mb model height \nanalysis showed an upper level high pressure over the southern \nstates and an upper level low pressure over southern Saskatchewan. \nAhead of the low pressure shortwave ridging extended through the \nwestern Great Lakes. Monsoonal moisture from the southwest U.S. was \nstreaming northeast into the midwest. Warm air advection had \ninitiated a line of showers and thunderstorms over the southern and \nwestern U.P. which was moving northeast. It was mild and humid with \ntemperatures and dewpoints generally in the mid 60s. Fog had \ndeveloped over the area, especially in favorable areas with east-\nsoutheast upslope flow such as the Keweenaw and higher elevation of \nthe north central UP. Behind the showers fog could become more \nwidespread and dense, will continue to monitor for the potential \nneed for fog headlines.\n\nThe upper level low pressure over southern Saskatchewan will move \neast to western Ontario by late tonight, weakening as it continues \nto occlude. Showers and thunderstorms will exit the area this \nmorning (a few heavy downpours can be expected as moisture is \nabundant, with PWATs over 1.5\"). Expect fog and low clouds to lift \nthrough mid-morning with some peaks of sun at times through the day. \nWith deep moisture in place and relatively steep mid-level lapse \nrates instability will be on the rise through the day, to as much as \n3000 J/kg over the south-central. Shear also increases through the \nday with 35-40kt shear by late this afternoon. The combination of \ninstability and shear will be sufficient for a strong to severe \nthunderstorm should a storm develop. However, large scale ascent is \nfairly weak and most models keep the atmosphere capped through the \nafternoon and evening, hindering thunderstorm development. SPC \nmaintains a marginal risk in the south-central for severe \nthunderstorms (~5% chance). The main hazards with any stronger \nstorms will be large hail, damaging winds and heavy rainfall. \nMaintained low pops, mainly over the south-central.\n\nIt will feel very warm and muggy today with highs reaching into the \nmid 80s and dewpoints well into the 60s to low 70s. Overnight \ntonight the deeper moisture will begin to shift east resulting in \nmore comfortable conditions as the night progresses, with lows in \nthe 50s west and low 60s east.\n\nThe forecast trends drier on Tuesday as Upper Michigan becomes \nsituated between low pressure over northern Ontario and high \npressure over the Tennessee Valley, which another day of highs in \nthe 80s expected. The ridge then reamplifies over the central CONUS \nfor the latter half of the week as the low over Ontario moves east \nto the Canadian Maritimes, placing the UP back into a northwest flow \nregime with temperature moderating into the 70s into next weekend. \nThis will favor generally drier weather through the end of the week, \nthough a few periods of showers can't be ruled out with any embedded \ndisturbances passing through the region.",
